Analyze the nature and structure of the forum, including its influence and legacy in post-Soviet political discourse
The Central Committee of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union (CPSU) served as the party's policy forum, playing a central role in shaping political and ideological direction. It functioned within the framework of democratic centralism, which emphasized strict subordination to higher party bodies and uncontested elections, ensuring that decisions were made by appointed leaders rather than general membership. This structure allowed the CPSU to maintain dominance over state institutions, as party members occupied key positions in government and society.

In post-Soviet political discourse, the legacy of this centralized, hierarchical system has influenced the political structures of former Soviet states. Some of these nations have adopted elements of "Neo-Sovietism," characterized by policies that emphasize state control, law and order, and centralized authority. This reflects a nostalgic or strategic revival of certain aspects of Soviet governance, particularly in countries like Russia under President Vladimir Putin, where centralized control and strong state presence are prominent features. The influence of the CPSU's structure can thus be seen in the persistence of single-party or dominant-party systems, as well as in the emphasis on state management of civil society and political life in some post-Soviet contexts.
