Identify the historical context and origins of the Black Seminole community, including their formation through intermarriage between enslaved Africans and Seminole Native Americans.
The Black Seminole community originated from the intermarriage between enslaved Africans and Seminole Native Americans, primarily in Spanish Florida. These individuals, often referred to as maroons, were escaped slaves who allied with Seminole groups. Over time, they formed distinct communities, blending African and Seminole cultural practices. They developed a syncretic form of Christianity and maintained traditions such as "jumping the broom" for marriage celebrations. While some Black Seminoles were held as slaves by Seminole leaders, they generally had more freedom than enslaved people under white ownership. Their society was influenced by both African heritage and Seminole matrilineal kinship systems.
