Research the historical context and significance of the material to confirm its invention and the role of the hairdresser in its development.
The historical context of the bouffant hairstyle and its significance in terms of invention and development can be traced back to the mid-20th century. The modern bouffant is considered to have been invented by British celebrity hairdresser Raymond Bessone. This style gained popularity in the early 1960s, largely due to its frequent appearance in fashion magazines and its adoption by prominent figures such as First Lady Jacqueline Kennedy. The term "bouffant" originates from the French word "bouffante," derived from "bouffer," meaning "to puff out." 

Regarding the role of hairpins in securing such hairstyles, hairpins have a long history dating back to ancient civilizations like Assyria and Egypt, where they were used both functionally and as ornamental objects. The spiral hairpin, a significant innovation, was invented in 1901 by New Zealand inventor Ernest Godward, contributing to the evolution of hairstyling tools and techniques. While this invention is not directly linked to the bouffant hairstyle, it highlights the broader development of tools that aid in creating and maintaining various hairstyles, including the bouffant.
