Identify the historical and legal context of the Mountbatten-Windsor name, including its adoption as a surname for male-line descendants of the Queen without royal styles and titles.
The Mountbatten-Windsor name was adopted as a surname for male-line descendants of Queen Elizabeth II and Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh, through a declaration made in the Privy Council in 1960. This name is specifically used by those without royal styles and titles. Individuals with royal styles typically do not use a surname, but some have used "Mountbatten-Windsor" when a surname was required. The name incorporates Prince Philip's adopted surname of Mountbatten and has been prescribed for direct descendants of Elizabeth II who lack royal titles.
