Determine which specific treaty or agreement officially ended this phase and explain its terms and significance in the context of the conflict.
The specific treaty that officially ended the first phase of the Second Anglo-Afghan War is the Treaty of Gandamak. Signed on 26 May 1879, it was concluded between King Mohammad Yaqub Khan of Afghanistan and Sir Louis Cavagnari, representing the British Government of India, at a British army camp near the village of Gandamak, east of Kabul. The treaty was ratified by Lord Edward Robert Bulwer Lytton, the Viceroy of India, on 30 May 1879.

The key terms of the Treaty of Gandamak included the cession of various frontier areas to Britain while allowing Afghanistan to retain full sovereignty over its territory. Additionally, the agreement classified the British agent and his staff in Kabul as Indian Muslims, which had implications for their status and treatment within Afghanistan.

In the context of the conflict, the Treaty of Gandamak marked the end of the first phase of the Second Anglo-Afghan War. It was seen as a prelude to the second phase of the war, as it did not fully satisfy British ambitions in the region and led to further tensions. The treaty's terms were controversial and contributed to the eventual breakdown of relations, culminating in the second phase of the conflict.
